WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.540
Hi everyone, in this lesson, we're gonna go ahead

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.500
and take a look at virtual shadow maps

3
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.199
and how to edit them and how they work overall

4
00:00:08.199 --> 00:00:09.539
when working with Lumen.

5
00:00:09.539 --> 00:00:11.369
So let's get started.

6
00:00:11.369 --> 00:00:14.410
So virtual shadow maps, what they do is update

7
00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.890
on the fly and they significantly increases

8
00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:22.190
shadow resolution to match highly detailed nanite geometry.

9
00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:26.140
Unlike shadow maps, which is the old baking method,

10
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.899
which are like texture projecting.

11
00:00:27.899 --> 00:00:30.899
So it's basically what you would do in the old way

12
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.700
of working with Unreal with lighting and baking,

13
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.020
you would bake your lights and the shadow would be baked

14
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.859
upon a static piece of geometry.

15
00:00:37.859 --> 00:00:39.960
And you would, if you move that geometry,

16
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.100
you would actually see that shadow still there

17
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.840
and you would have to rebake for it to update

18
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:51.000
on a static light in your environment and static geometry.

19
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.990
So plausible soft shadows with reasonable,

20
00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:59.270
controllable performance causes what it gives you.

21
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.229
Provide a simple solution, it provides that,

22
00:01:02.509 --> 00:01:04.790
that works by default with limited amounts

23
00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:05.989
of adjustment needed.

24
00:01:05.989 --> 00:01:07.909
So out of the box, it works really well.

25
00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:09.829
I'm gonna give you a few tips and tricks

26
00:01:09.829 --> 00:01:13.090
to be able to adjust those shadows if you need to,

27
00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:14.750
but out of the box, it works really well

28
00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.510
and you'll probably very rare occasions

29
00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.250
have to edit things.

30
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.150
Replace the many stationary light shadowing techniques

31
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.709
with a single unified path.

32
00:01:25.709 --> 00:01:26.950
So in the old days,

33
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.810
we'd have multiple stationary lights.

34
00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.810
Basically they allowed your object to move

35
00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:33.930
and in that object moving,

36
00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.530
you would get some soft degrading shadows

37
00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.590
and you would have to rebake anytime you adjusted

38
00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.489
some of your static objects.

39
00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:46.209
And it would just, it was a lot of work

40
00:01:46.209 --> 00:01:49.209
and it was a simplistic type of answer

41
00:01:51.469 --> 00:01:54.549
to working with Unreal, but now working with Lumen,

42
00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:56.569
everything can be updated on the fly.

43
00:01:56.569 --> 00:01:59.010
Now, remember your lights need to be movable

44
00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:00.689
when you're setting this and you need to make sure

45
00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:02.549
that your virtual shadow maps are on.

46
00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:05.329
By default, they do come on, but if you're inheriting,

47
00:02:05.329 --> 00:02:07.569
if you're inheriting an older scene,

48
00:02:07.569 --> 00:02:08.770
you need to double check that

49
00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:10.090
and make sure that they're on.

50
00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.969
And you'll see this here where you can actually

51
00:02:11.969 --> 00:02:13.770
look it up in your project settings

52
00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.090
and simply just make sure your virtual shadow maps

53
00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.530
are on.

54
00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.650
So again, Lumen is made for virtual shadow maps.

55
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.250
Virtual shadows do not work in a baking scenario.

56
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.169
You would have to set things to light maps.

57
00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.889
So it's not intended for Unreal standard baking.

58
00:02:29.889 --> 00:02:31.250
If you want to switch to baking,

59
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:35.400
you must change your shadow settings, as you see here.

60
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.560
Let's go ahead and move on.

61
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.039
So break things down, to break things out a little

62
00:02:40.039 --> 00:02:43.819
further, virtual textures deal with similar issues.

63
00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:48.599
Virtual shadow maps handles shadows similarly.

64
00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:51.680
And what that means is it only calls up,

65
00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.340
brings up the visual fidelity,

66
00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.919
the highest quality pieces of those shadows.

67
00:02:58.439 --> 00:02:59.280
You can think of it like textures,

68
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.240
but of the shadow,

69
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.189
when your camera is in that particular region or area.

70
00:03:05.189 --> 00:03:08.770
So virtual textures splits the texture

71
00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:13.389
and MIP chain into tiles and streams it in only,

72
00:03:13.389 --> 00:03:15.090
when the only ones needed.

73
00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.050
So it's kind of the same thing

74
00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:19.409
when it comes to virtual shadow maps.

75
00:03:19.409 --> 00:03:20.990
Local lights, you'll see here,

76
00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:25.990
it says 16K by 16K virtual resolution with eight MIPs.

77
00:03:26.229 --> 00:03:30.379
Spotlights use a single virtual shadow map.

78
00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:32.680
Point light, point and rec lights,

79
00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:37.810
each use six virtual shadow maps, one per cubic face.

80
00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:42.780
2K more than conventional shadow maps in Unreal, baked.

81
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.139
Can still run out if you make massive local lights.

82
00:03:46.139 --> 00:03:50.439
Now that has changed so that now we have mega lights.

83
00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:51.900
Now we get into mega lights

84
00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.539
in our atmospheric lighting course.

85
00:03:53.539 --> 00:03:56.219
Now remember, it's still experimental in beta,

86
00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:59.180
but that allows you to not hit that cap so quickly

87
00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.180
and hit that performance cap

88
00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.099
so that you can actually add as much as you need.

89
00:04:04.099 --> 00:04:06.780
A lights and your geometry work really nice together.

90
00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.560
But again, it is experimental,

91
00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.740
but it is way better than the old baking method

92
00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:15.020
and your visual fidelity will come up as you need it

93
00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.720
based on the position of your camera in the world.

